  "sourceSolutions": [
    {
      "source": "1085",
      "task": "Prove arbitrarily many distinct other natural-number solution pairs, not just another witness.",
      "solution": "The equation is x^2-2y^2=1. Starting at(x1,y1)=(3,2), define x(n+1)=3x(n)+4y(n) and y(n+1)=2x(n)+3y(n), obtained by multiplying x+y*sqrt2 by3+2*sqrt2. Direct expansion gives(3x+4y)^2-2(2x+3y)^2=x^2-2y^2. Positive integer pairs stay positive integers and x(n+1)>x(n),y(n+1)>y(n), so all pairs are distinct. For n>=2 these are other pairs, beginning(17,12),(99,70); induction provides arbitrarily many. This proof does not assume that0 belongs to N."
    },
    {
      "source": "1087",
      "task": "Determine every value of the named linear coefficient in the quadratic.",
      "solution": "For roots r,s, r+s=-p and rs=1. Hence r^2+s^2=(r+s)^2-2rs=p^2-2=254, giving p^2=256 and p=16 or-16. Both have discriminant252>0, so both are valid even under real-root interpretation. Uppercase P in the final prose refers to the coefficient named lowercase p in the equation; no new coefficient is introduced and the original bytes remain unchanged.",
      "sourceQualification": "Original p/P spelling discrepancy retained; the equation has only one parameter."
    },
    {
      "source": "1089",
      "task": "Prove the square maximizes area among all rectangles of the same positive perimeter.",
      "solution": "For sides a,b>0 and fixed perimeter P=2(a+b), a+b=P/2. Area ab=[(a+b)^2-(a-b)^2]/4=P^2/16-(a-b)^2/4<=P^2/16. Equality iff a=b=P/4, precisely the square. Thus the bound is attained, not merely approached, and no nonsquare has the same maximum."
    },
    {
      "source": "1090",
      "printedSourceNumber": "1030",
      "task": "Prove the prime-or-one remainder assertion for every prime, including primes2,3,5.",
      "solution": "For p=2,3,5 the remainder upon division by30 is respectively2,3,5, already prime. For p>5, p is coprime to30. Writing p=30q+r gives0<=r<30 and gcd(r,30)=1. The only such r are1,7,11,13,17,19,23,29; each except1 is prime. Therefore every prime has a prime remainder or1. The original printed1030/logical1090 mapping is preserved, not repaired into a new identifier."
    },
    {
      "source": "1091",
      "task": "Determine the downstream raft travel time from the two powered-boat travel times.",
      "solution": "Let D>0 be the distance, u the boat's still-water speed and w>0 the uniform current speed. u+w=D/8 and u-w=D/10. Subtracting gives2w=D/8-D/10=D/40, so w=D/80. A passive raft moves with current and takes D/w=80 hours. Also u=9D/80>w; the powered-boat times are consistent. Using1/8-1/10 as the current rate would omit the factor2."
    },
    {
      "source": "1092",
      "task": "Determine the amounts of the two zinc/copper alloys needed for composition17:27, and distinguish amount units.",
      "solution": "If X,Y are masses of the first and second alloys measured in the same unit, their zinc fractions are1/3 and2/5, copper fractions2/3 and3/5. Required zinc fraction is17/44, so X/3+2Y/5=17(X+Y)/44. Rearrangement gives(-7/132)X+(3/220)Y=0 and X:Y=9:35. Taking masses9 and35 gives zinc3+14=17 and copper6+21=27. Alternatively, a composition batch of the first alloy contains one common mass unit zinc and two copper(total3); a batch of the second contains two zinc and three copper(total5). Three first batches and seven second batches give zinc3+14=17,copper6+21=27 and again total alloy masses9:35.",
      "sourceQualification": "Original 'parts' does not separately define whether it means common total-mass units or whole composition batches of unequal total masses. The physical required alloy mass ratio is9:35. The batch-count representation3:7 refers to explicitly defined batches of masses3 and5, not equal-mass portions. A conditional view must not claim one of these unit definitions was explicit in the original prose."
    },
    {
      "source": "1093",
      "task": "Determine the distance using first meeting and second meeting after both endpoint turnarounds.",
      "solution": "Let distance be D. At the first meeting the rider from B traveled50 and the rider from A traveled D-50, so speed ratio vA:vB=(D-50):50. At the second meeting25 from A, their total distances are2D-25 and D+25. Their sum is3D, three times the first meeting's total D; with fixed speeds, second-meeting elapsed time is three times the first. Thus the rider from B traveled3*50=150=D+25 and D=125 km. Check the other rider: first75 and second225=2*125-25, speed ratio3:2. Both have passed their opposite endpoints and are returning at the specified second meeting."
    },
    {
      "source": "1094",
      "task": "Find all physically admissible horse speeds satisfying overtaking, arrival at N, immediate return and second meeting11/4 hours after overtaking.",
      "solution": "Let horse speed be v>5 km/h. When the horse starts, walker leads10 km. Catch time measured after horse start is t=10/(v-5); catch position is10+5t km from M. During the following11/4 hours the walker advances55/4 km. Horse goes from catch position to41 and back to catch position+55/4, traveling82-2(10+5t)-55/4=193/4-10t. Thus(11/4)v=193/4-10t, yielding11v^2-248v+1365=0. Discriminant is1444=38^2, giving v=13 or105/11 km/h. Both are physical: for13,t=5/4,catch position65/4, second position30, and horse travel143/4=13*11/4; for105/11,t=11/5,catch position21, second position139/4, and horse travel105/4=(105/11)*11/4. Both catch and second positions are below41, the horse reaches41 before the second meeting, and walker does not reach N first. Thus no valid root is discarded because the source uses singular wording.",
      "wordingQualification": "Original singular request does not make speed unique. Preserve both admissible positive roots and do not replace the given timing."
    }
  ],
